Here's a step-by-step guide to create a Flask web app that shows a notification when the webcam is active:

### 1. Project Setup
Create a new directory and install Flask:
```bash
mkdir flask-camera-notification
cd flask-camera-notification
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: venv\Scripts\activate
pip install flask
```

### 2. Create Flask Application
**app.py**:
```python
from flask import Flask, render_template, jsonify, request

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def index():
    return render_template('index.html')

@app.route('/camera_status', methods=['POST'])
def camera_status():
    status = request.json.get('status')
    if status:
        print("Camera is ON")
        return jsonify({"message": "Notification: Camera is active!"})
    return jsonify({"message": ""})

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(debug=True)
```

### 3. Create HTML Template
**templates/index.html**:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Camera Notification</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Webcam Access</h1>
    <video id="video" width="640" height="480" autoplay></video>
    <div id="notification"></div>

    <script>
        // Access webcam
        const video = document.getElementById('video');
        const notification = document.getElementById('notification');
        
        navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ video: true })
            .then(stream => {
                video.srcObject = stream;
                
                // Send status to Flask
                fetch('/camera_status', {
                    method: 'POST',
                    headers: {
                        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
                    },
                    body: JSON.stringify({ status: true })
                })
                .then(response => response.json())
                .then(data => {
                    if (data.message) {
                        notification.textContent = data.message;
                        notification.style.color = 'red';
                        notification.style.padding = '10px';
                    }
                });
            })
            .catch(error => {
                console.error('Error accessing camera:', error);
            });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### 4. Run the Application
```bash
python app.py
```

Visit `http://localhost:5000` in your browser. When you allow camera access:
1. The browser will show the camera feed
2. A red notification will appear saying "Notification: Camera is active!"
3. The server will print "Camera is ON" in the console

### Key Features:
- Camera access using WebRTC API
- POST request to Flask when camera activates
- Visual notification in the browser
- Server-side logging

### Security Notes:
1. Works only over HTTPS in production (required for camera access)
2. Needs user permission to access camera
3. Only shows notification for initial activation (you can add more logic for continuous monitoring)

To enhance this, you could:
